WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:11.760
On the 22nd of July 1943, the US 7th Army captured&nbsp;
Palermo, the largest city on Sicily, after General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:22.400
George Patton’s forces had taken 100 miles of Axis&nbsp;
territory in 72 hours. Patton was relentless and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:29.040
continued charging about Sicily, conquering towns&nbsp;
and capturing tens of thousands of prisoners of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:37.440
war. In response, three days after the fall of&nbsp;
Palermo, the Grand Council of Fascism removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:46.880
Benito Mussolini from power. After Sicily, Patton&nbsp;
was the most feared Allied commander in Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:54.800
So why was this tactically brilliant general&nbsp;
sidelined when the D-Day campaign took place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:15.520
a year later? This is the story of George Patton,&nbsp;
a brilliant general and utterly flawed man.
&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:25.920
The man known to history as George Smith Patton&nbsp;
Jnr. was born on the 11th of November 1885, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:34.000
San Gabriel in the state of California. Although&nbsp;
he was generally known as George Jnr., he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:44.400
theoretically George Patton III, as his father and&nbsp;
grandfather both had the same name as him.
&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:51.440
His father, George, was an attorney, businessman&nbsp;
and politician who served for three months as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:02:00.080
District Attorney of Los Angeles County in 1887&nbsp;
and much later as the Mayor of San Marino. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:06.000
family had a military background and young&nbsp;
George’s grandfather had died fighting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.840
the Confederacy during the American Civil War.&nbsp;
George Jnr.’s mother was Ruth Wilson, the daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.960
of one of California’s wealthiest men, Benjamin&nbsp;
Davis Wilson, a businessman who had moved out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:27.360
California when it was still under Mexican rule&nbsp;
and who subsequently became the second Mayor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:36.160
Los Angeles in the middle of the gold rush after&nbsp;
the region was annexed by the US in 1848. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:43.280
Ruth married George Patton Snr. they lived in San&nbsp;
Gabriel on the Lake Vineyard estate owned by her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:51.840
father. They had two children, George Jnr., and&nbsp;
his younger sister Anne, who was known as Nita.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:58.560
The Patton family were descended from the Lords&nbsp;
of Glamorgan, a Welsh family, as well as from King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:06.240
Edward I of England, while they had Scottish and&nbsp;
French Huguenot ancestry and were tied to a branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:14.800
of the family of George Washington. 
	
George Jnr. was tutored at home until he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.120
eleven years old. His literacy skills were&nbsp;
consequently under-developed when he began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:28.320
attending Stephen Clark’s School for boys, a&nbsp;
private school in Pasadena. Once he did learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:35.200
to read, he did so avidly and became especially&nbsp;
interested in books on military history and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:42.720
commanders such as Julius Caesar, the Carthaginian&nbsp;
general Hannibal and Napoleon Bonaparte.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.520
He also read the works of John Singleton Mosby,&nbsp;
a Confederate cavalry commander known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:56.480
“Gray Ghost” on account of his ability to&nbsp;
attack the enemy and then seemingly vanish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:02.560
The Pattons’ own extended legacy as a military&nbsp;
family, with many relatives who fought in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:09.280
American Revolutionary War and the Civil War,&nbsp;
left an impression on George from a young age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:16.480
and became an important part of his identity.&nbsp;
It was unsurprising, then, that at seventeen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:24.400
years of age George set off across the country to&nbsp;
enrol in the Virginia Military Institute in 1903,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:30.880
an institution which both his father and&nbsp;
grandfather had attended before him. He was later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:38.720
nominated to the military academy at West Point.&nbsp;
This was already established as the elite training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:47.760
school for future military officers in the US.&nbsp;
Alumni included Ulysses S. Grant, George Custer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.360
“Stonewall” Jackson and John Pershing, the latter&nbsp;
of whom would soon play a role in Patton’s career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:02.320
In some ways Patton was well suited to life at&nbsp;
West Point, but he struggled at mathematics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.440
a subject that was considered increasingly&nbsp;
important at the academy in a time when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:14.640
military logistics were becoming central&nbsp;
to military command. He excelled at sports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:21.600
as he was already an accomplished horseman and&nbsp;
competed in track and field events too. He joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:31.280
the football team but after injuring his arm&nbsp;
he moved over to the fencing team instead.
&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:37.840
After his initial difficulties, George did well&nbsp;
at West Point and was made a cadet sergeant major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:46.960
in his junior year and an adjutant in his senior&nbsp;
year. After graduating, he left in June 1909 after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:53.920
being commissioned as a second lieutenant assigned&nbsp;
to the 15th Cavalry Division at Fort Sheridan in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:04.080
Illinois. Later he was transferred to Fort Myer in&nbsp;
Virginia. On the 26th of May 1910, Patton married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:11.520
Beatrice Banning Ayer, the daughter of a Boston&nbsp;
industrialist, Frederick Ayer, at Beverly Farms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:20.400
Massachusetts. They went on to have three children&nbsp;
together, Beatrice, Ruth and George Patton IV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:27.680
While still serving at Fort Myer as quartermaster&nbsp;
of his troop in 1911, George befriended Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:35.680
Stimson, the Secretary of War, whose aide he&nbsp;
became. George also continued as a fencer and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:42.000
skill led to him being sent to the participate&nbsp;
at the Summer Olympics in Stockholm in 1912,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:48.560
where he finished in 5th place overall in the&nbsp;
modern pentathlon, which consisted of swimming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:56.560
pistol shooting, running, fencing and riding.&nbsp;
Patton was the highest placed of the non-Swedish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:03.760
participants. He was among the top five performers&nbsp;
in fencing, swimming and horse-riding, but he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:11.760
placed in 20th in the shooting event. Controversy&nbsp;
arose over the scoring for George’s shooting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:20.000
as he had used a .38 caliber pistol, whilst&nbsp;
other competitors were using .22 calibre pistols.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:26.480
George argued that some of his shots must have&nbsp;
passed straight through the previous holes he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:32.720
had made in the score card because of the larger&nbsp;
impact holes that the higher calibre pistol had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:39.360
made. The adjudicators disagreed and concluded&nbsp;
that some of his shots had missed the target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:46.800
completely. If his argument was correct, he&nbsp;
would likely have medalled at the Olympics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.560
He returned home via France, where he&nbsp;
studied fencing under Sir Charles Cléry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:08:00.880
a Master at Arms of the French Cavalry Academy. On&nbsp;
his return to the US, Patton designed a new sword,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:10.400
the Model 1913 Cavalry Saber, known as the ‘Patton&nbsp;
Saber’. In 1913, an order was placed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:18.560
military for 20,000 of these blades. Later he was&nbsp;
made ‘Master of the Sword’, the most prestigious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:28.480
title offered to swordsmanship instructors at Fort&nbsp;
Riley in Kansas, where he was then stationed.
&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.480
Patton attended the Mounted Service School after&nbsp;
returning to the US and graduated in the summer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:41.920
of 1915. He was assigned to the 15th Cavalry,&nbsp;
which was bound for the Philippines, an American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:48.640
colony at the time, but before he was sent to&nbsp;
Southeast Asia he persuaded influential contacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:57.040
in Washington to intervene to have him re-assigned&nbsp;
to the 8th Cavalry instead at Fort Bliss in Texas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:04.400
as he feared a post in the Philippines would prove&nbsp;
to be a career dead-end. Instead, with the Mexican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:10.400
Revolution underway throughout the 1910s, and&nbsp;
the war threatening to spill over the border into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:18.400
places like Texas, Patton viewed assignment to&nbsp;
Fort Bliss as preferential over the Philippines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:26.320
In this way, Patton’s first experience of active&nbsp;
military combat came in 1916 when he took part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:33.440
in the Pancho Villa Expedition, the US army’s&nbsp;
campaign against the paramilitary forces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:39.520
Mexican revolutionary Francisco ‘Pancho’&nbsp;
Villa, whose men had attacked the border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:48.800
town of Columbus in New Mexico on the 9th of March&nbsp;
1916, along with nearby Camp Furlong. In response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.080
General Frederick Funston, leader of the 8th&nbsp;
Cavalry, who was stationed at Fort Bliss, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:10:02.080
ordered to secure the Mexico-United States border&nbsp;
and to pursue Villa and his men. Patton’s unit was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:08.880
not part of the force, and so George appealed to&nbsp;
commander John Pershing to serve as his personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:16.480
aide in the campaign, acting as a courier and&nbsp;
helping to plan the action along the border area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:23.840
George’s eagerness and dedication to the task&nbsp;
at hand impressed Pershing and in April 1916&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:30.480
he appointed Patton to command troop C of the&nbsp;
13th Cavalry, the unit that was tasked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:39.600
hunting down Villa and his subordinates. Thus,&nbsp;
on the 14th of May 1916 Patton first saw combat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:46.000
a date also famous today for the first use&nbsp;
of motorised transport in American military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:54.720
history as cavalry units began to diversify away&nbsp;
from horses. However, the overall expedition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:02.560
which ended in February 1917, was ultimately&nbsp;
unsuccessful, as they failed in their main goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:08.080
which was to capture ‘Pancho’ Villa,&nbsp;
instead killing several of his followers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:16.240
including Colonel Candelario Cervantes, General&nbsp;
Francisco Beltran and Villa’s second in command,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:24.320
Julio Cardenas. At one point during his mission&nbsp;
Patton and his subordinates, in a trio of Dodge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:31.760
touring cars, encountered a number of Villa’s men,&nbsp;
who were foraging for food. According to accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:38.960
Patton shot three of the bandits. Patton’s brash&nbsp;
approach to military command, where he had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:46.000
reservations about putting himself in harm’s&nbsp;
way, gained him both admiration and notoriety.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.680
The American press labelled him as the&nbsp;
‘bandit-killer’ shortly afterwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.680 --> 00:12:00.000
Patton was promoted to the rank of first&nbsp;
lieutenant after this excursion after Villa.
&nbsp;

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:07.360
For several months Pershing, Patton and their men&nbsp;
remained on the Mexico border conducting training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:14.240
However, even though the civil war dragged on&nbsp;
in Mexico, the focus of US military activity was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:23.680
soon drawn elsewhere to Europe after the US entry&nbsp;
into the First World War in April 1917. Pershing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:29.680
with Patton in tow, was appointed to command the&nbsp;
advance American Expeditionary Force that was sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:37.360
to Western Europe while wider war mobilisation was&nbsp;
underway at home. Patton personally requested to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:44.080
join Pershing in France and his request was&nbsp;
granted, resulting in him being promoted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:51.200
Captain, after which he joined Pershing in the&nbsp;
advance party of officers that headed to Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:57.200
There he worked as Pershing’s personal aide,&nbsp;
overseeing the last minute training of American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:04.960
troops, who were steadily arriving in their tens&nbsp;
of thousands. After overseeing this work in Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:12.640
in September 1917 Patton was reassigned to&nbsp;
Chaumont as post adjutant, being placed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:19.360
charge of the company that ran the American base.&nbsp;
Patton was not temperamentally suited to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:29.040
position and he complained to Pershing, asking&nbsp;
him to reassign him to front line duties.
&nbsp;

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:34.400
It was during this period that Patton first&nbsp;
encountered the piece of military hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:42.240
that would revolutionise both his own strategic&nbsp;
thinking and the entire nature of modern warfare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:50.240
The first tanks were developed and deployed&nbsp;
in 1916 by the British as a pioneering means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:58.080
of reducing the lethality of machine guns on&nbsp;
the Western Front. By placing men inside what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:05.680
was basically a giant, moving metal box, it was&nbsp;
envisaged that the stalemate created by trench&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:13.440
warfare could be broken. Soon the Germans, and in&nbsp;
particular the French, were developing what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:20.880
come to be described as armoured cavalry. After a&nbsp;
bout of sick leave as he recovered from jaundice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:27.040
Patton made the acquaintance of Colonel Fox&nbsp;
Connor, an operations officer with the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:33.680
Expeditionary Force in Paris, who encouraged&nbsp;
Patton to seek a position commanding one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:41.760
new tank regiments. Consequently, on the 10th&nbsp;
of November 1917, Patton was assigned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:48.240
American Expeditionary Force Light Tank School.&nbsp;
While he was fascinated with the possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:55.280
that the new machines offered, he had to acquaint&nbsp;
himself with the nuances of the hardware. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.280 --> 00:15:03.200
therefore attached to the French Tank School&nbsp;
where he first drove a Renault FT light tank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:11.280
After doing so, he became convinced that these&nbsp;
new weapons were the future of terrestrial combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:16.960
Once he heard that the British were planning&nbsp;
a mass attack of tanks, on the German line at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:23.760
Cambrai, which proved to be the largest use of&nbsp;
these vehicles thus far, Patton arranged to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:30.160
Colonel J. F. C. Fuller, the Chief of Staff&nbsp;
of the British Tank Corps, in order to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:39.920
more about how to incorporate the metallic&nbsp;
weapons of war into the US armed forces.
&nbsp;

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:46.240
On his way back to Paris after meeting with&nbsp;
Fuller not far from Cambrai, Patton stopped at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:53.120
the Renault factory to watch tanks being assembled&nbsp;
there. He was promoted to the rank of Major in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.120 --> 00:16:00.640
January of 1918. Less than a month later, the&nbsp;
Americans were given ten tanks by the French to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:07.920
use at their training school at Bourg. As the only&nbsp;
relatively senior American officer to have driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:15.440
one of these, Patton was assigned to instruct&nbsp;
others in how to use these armoured vehicles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:21.360
At the same time, he was trying to convince&nbsp;
his superiors within the American military that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:29.280
the use of the tanks on the battlefield was the&nbsp;
future. This was easier said than done, as at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:37.040
early stage in their development, these were still&nbsp;
extremely slow and often mechanically unreliable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:44.480
Many within the American Expeditionary Force&nbsp;
believed they had limited utility. Undaunted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:51.120
Patton tenaciously continued to press his case,&nbsp;
and it wasn’t long before tanks were beginning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:57.680
be incorporated into the American army’s battle&nbsp;
plans for the closing stages of the conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:05.920
In tandem, Patton was promoted once again, in&nbsp;
April 1918, to the rank of Lieutenant Colonel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:12.640
He took part on the 12th of September 1918 during&nbsp;
the Saint-Mihiel offensive and again on the 26th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:18.640
of the month in the Meuse-Argonne offensive,&nbsp;
where he was injured whilst storming the German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:26.240
lines after his tanks had become isolated and cut&nbsp;
off from the main attack. Though the bullet wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:33.600
in his left leg was serious, Patton commanded the&nbsp;
attack from the safety of a shell-hole for over an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:40.640
hour before being rescued, insisting on personally&nbsp;
submitting his report of the battle to the command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:47.920
post before going to the field hospital where&nbsp;
he was treated for the injury. The wound kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:55.920
Patton out of the fighting until the war ended&nbsp;
on the 11th of November 1918. Despite not having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.920 --> 00:18:02.720
experienced much direct combat in the Great War,&nbsp;
Patton had helped to demonstrate to the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:12.160
forces the potential power of tank warfare and&nbsp;
that he was a commander with innovative ideas.
&nbsp;

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:20.560
The inter-war years were not a particularly happy&nbsp;
time for Patton, as he became agitated by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:27.760
extended peace and the lack of opportunities for&nbsp;
an active command. In the interim he continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:35.760
to push for the expanded incorporation of tank&nbsp;
divisions into the military. In 1919 he was sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:43.200
to Washington D.C. to join a committee dedicated&nbsp;
to writing a manual on tank operations for future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:51.040
conflicts. At this juncture he began arguing that&nbsp;
tanks should have their own armoured unit, rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:57.840
than fulfilling the role of simply supporting&nbsp;
infantry divisions. Yet, while his push for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:04.400
development of independent armoured divisions was&nbsp;
supported by his friend, Dwight D. Eisenhower, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:11.760
the Secretary of War, Dwight Davis, there was no&nbsp;
appetite in Washington for funding such a costly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:19.840
initiative now that the war was over. As a result,&nbsp;
the first armoured corps within the US army would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:29.120
not be developed until 1940. Patton moved between&nbsp;
various posts in the years that followed. In 1924,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:37.120
he attended the General staff college, where&nbsp;
he graduated 25th out of a school of 248.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:43.360
In the summer of 1932, in the midst of the&nbsp;
economic crisis of the Great Depression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:50.800
over 17,000 World War One veterans converged&nbsp;
on the capital in protest over the failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:59.360
of the government to convert their wartime&nbsp;
bonuses into cash. On the 28th of July 1932,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:05.760
this protest turned violent when the police&nbsp;
attempted to break up the demonstration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:14.960
Patton was ordered to march with 600 men against&nbsp;
the protestors however he was reluctant to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:21.840
having considerable sympathy for the veterans’&nbsp;
cause. Nevertheless, he obeyed orders and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:31.200
so-called ‘Bonus Army’ was driven away. At the&nbsp;
start of 1935 Patton was transferred to Hawaii.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:36.640
While he was there, he observed the growing&nbsp;
expansion of the Japanese Empire in the Far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:43.920
East and the large Japanese community on the&nbsp;
Hawaiian islands. He therefore developed a plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:51.680
to detain the Japanese people living in Hawaii in&nbsp;
the event of an invasion. This was controversial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:58.000
though it ultimately became government policy&nbsp;
after the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:08.240
December 1941. In 1937, as the Japanese invaded&nbsp;
China, Patton also correctly predicted a potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:13.600
surprise attack on the American Pacific&nbsp;
Fleet by the Japanese in future years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:24.800
in a paper he wrote entitled ‘Surprise’. 
As the peacetime lull continued and Patton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:31.680
became bored he developed a drinking problem&nbsp;
and even began an extra-marital affair with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:39.840
niece-in-law, Jean Gordon, when he was 49 and&nbsp;
she was 21, although there is a debate among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:46.880
historians as to whether or not this affair&nbsp;
really took place. Some biographies argue it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:53.600
was merely a rumour without any evidence to&nbsp;
support it. The end of his long hiatus from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.600 --> 00:22:01.680
active war came nearer on the 1st of September&nbsp;
1939, when Nazi Germany invaded Poland and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:08.720
Second World War began in Europe. Although the&nbsp;
US was not initially involved in the conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:15.360
it began to mobilise as a precaution and Patton&nbsp;
worked to ensure that this mobilisation included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:23.520
the building up of independent armoured divisions.&nbsp;
In 1940 the 1st and 2nd armoured divisions were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:31.920
created. On the 4th of April 1941 Patton was&nbsp;
promoted to the rank of Major-General. Patton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:39.680
also staged a practice exercise in which 1,000&nbsp;
tanks and armoured vehicles raced from the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:46.880
of Georgia to Florida. To increase his tactical&nbsp;
knowledge, Patton earned a pilot’s license,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:52.960
so that he could observe these exercises from&nbsp;
the sky and thus gain a better insight into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.960 --> 00:23:00.560
movements of armoured units across long distances.&nbsp;
These huge exercises in the southeast of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:07.680
country gained considerable attention, captivating&nbsp;
the nation with Patton’s new method of warfare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:16.160
Patton also developed a training centre in the&nbsp;
deserts of California in 1941, aiming to simulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:25.360
the conditions of the North African Desert, which&nbsp;
had developed into an Allied theatre of war.
&nbsp;

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:33.120
On the 7th of December 1941, just as Patton&nbsp;
had predicted four years earlier, the Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:40.320
launched a devastating surprise assault on the&nbsp;
American naval base at Pearl Harbour in Hawaii.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:48.880
The US declared war on Japan the following day&nbsp;
and on Japan’s ally, Germany, three days later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:55.040
forming the Allied Alliance with Britain,&nbsp;
the Soviet Union and other powers like Canada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.040 --> 00:24:02.800
and the Free French. On the 15th of January&nbsp;
1942, Patton was placed in command of the 1st&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:09.760
armoured corps and immediately began stepping up&nbsp;
training exercises in the desert training centre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:17.600
Patton’s speed and aggression defined his command&nbsp;
style and his military career as a whole. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:24.400
sense he was very similar to the Axis General,&nbsp;
Erwin Rommel, who had dominated the German command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:31.280
in the North African theatre. Perhaps it was not&nbsp;
surprising then that Patton’s first major command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:38.400
in the Second World War was in North Africa, where&nbsp;
his friend, Lieutenant General Dwight Eisenhower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:46.160
assigned Patton to help plan the US invasion&nbsp;
of North Africa, known as Operation Torch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:52.320
This was intended to support the British forces&nbsp;
already stationed in Egypt and to push out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.320 --> 00:25:00.960
Germans and Italians while also subduing the Vichy&nbsp;
French forces in Algeria and Morocco. Patton was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:08.480
given command of the western taskforce, some&nbsp;
33,000 men, who he ordered to begin landings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:16.400
centred on Casablanca in Morocco on the 8th&nbsp;
of November 1942. Before these landings began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:23.120
Patton addressed his troops in a way which&nbsp;
epitomised his military philosophy, stating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:33.360
“We shall attack and attack, until we are&nbsp;
exhausted, and then we shall attack again.”
&nbsp;

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:39.600
Despite meeting staunch resistance from the Vichy&nbsp;
French forces, Patton’s men gained a beachhead in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:46.480
Morocco and pressed on to Casablanca, which&nbsp;
fell on the 11th of November. Patton then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:52.640
turned this into a military port which acted&nbsp;
as an entry-point henceforth for further Allied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.640 --> 00:26:00.000
supplies and reinforcements for the North Africa&nbsp;
campaign. A conference of the Allied leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:06.800
US President Franklin Delano Roosevelt and British&nbsp;
Prime Minister Winston Churchill, was held here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:16.880
in January 1943. However, US forces suffered a&nbsp;
setback weeks later when the 2nd Corps under Major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:24.320
General Frendendall was defeated at the Kasserine&nbsp;
Pass in Tunisia by Field Marshal Rommel’s Afrika&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:31.600
Korps. The poorly led American troops were pushed&nbsp;
back 50 miles by Rommel’s experienced veterans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:38.560
taking heavy casualties along the way. Patton&nbsp;
responded by removing Frendendall from command,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:45.680
heightening military discipline across US forces,&nbsp;
pushing his troops hard, and instilling fear and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:52.720
discipline into the men. In response the men&nbsp;
nicknamed Patton ‘Old Blood and Guts’ due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:59.840
his lust for battle and harsh command-style.&nbsp;
Nonetheless, Patton was an inspiring figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:05.840
for his troops, setting an example for them&nbsp;
with his theatrical image, wearing a highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:12.560
polished helmet with cavalry boots and brandishing&nbsp;
a pair of ivory-handled pistols, a flashy bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:20.720
behaviour he had adopted all the way back in 1916&nbsp;
after he acquired one of these guns in New Mexico.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:28.000
He also travelled in a vehicle bearing his rank&nbsp;
insignia in large letters with sirens blaring from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:36.400
the truck to indicate he was unafraid of enemy&nbsp;
snipers. Patton’s measures helped US forces to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:44.240
swiftly regain the offensive, and on the 17th&nbsp;
of March 1943 they captured the Tunisian city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:53.840
of Gafsa in the battle of El Guettar. 
With the situation in Tunisia improving for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.840 --> 00:28:01.040
Allies, Eisenhower recalled Patton in April to&nbsp;
take control of American forces for Operation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:08.800
Husky, the planned Allied invasion of Sicily&nbsp;
to open a Southern Front in Europe. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:16.000
scheduled for July 1943, however Patton was&nbsp;
frustrated as the British commanders clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:22.880
viewed the Americans as inexperienced and&nbsp;
tried to side-line his 7th Army in favour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:29.920
of British General Bernard Montgomery leading&nbsp;
the expedition. Patton soon proved them wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:36.880
After the Allies landed in Sicily on the 10th of&nbsp;
July, Montgomery’s forces met strong resistance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:43.440
in response to which Patton charged onwards&nbsp;
with his men and captured the Sicilian capital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:51.600
Palermo. Then, with Montgomery’s advance slowed&nbsp;
by strong Axis resistance, Patton led his forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:59.600
deeper into Sicily and reached the northeastern&nbsp;
port of Messina on the 17th of August. With this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:06.640
advance, Patton’s armoured forces had thus&nbsp;
achieved a lightning quick success in Sicily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:14.400
inflicting over 110,000 casualties on the Italians&nbsp;
and Germans, mostly in captured prisoners of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:21.040
war. Patton had distinguished himself in this&nbsp;
campaign as one of the finest Allied commanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:29.600
but his fierce temper threatened to undermine his&nbsp;
military success. Unlike many other commanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:36.880
George Patton made frequent visits to combat&nbsp;
hospitals, seeing it as, a general’s duty to view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.880 --> 00:29:44.560
his wounded troops. On the 3rd of August Patton&nbsp;
came across an infantryman in a hospital with no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:52.240
visible injuries. After questioning him, Patton&nbsp;
erupted and slapped the soldier, pushing him out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:59.200
of the hospital tent. A week later he repeated&nbsp;
this behaviour with another soldier and accused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.200 --> 00:30:06.000
him of cowardice. Eisenhower reprimanded Patton&nbsp;
for these incidents and attempted to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:14.800
the story being released to the American public.&nbsp;
However, it was leaked in November 1943, causing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:22.880
furious reaction from Congress and the public, and&nbsp;
greatly denting Patton’s domestic reputation.
&nbsp;

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:30.880
Patton’s behaviour had raised enough questions&nbsp;
about his temperament that his subordinate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:38.160
Omar Bradley, was seen thereafter as being more&nbsp;
reliable and was chosen to lead the US 1st Army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:45.440
in the upcoming invasion of Nazi-occupied France.&nbsp;
This does not mean that Patton’s services were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:53.280
not availed of. The Allied command knew that the&nbsp;
Germans respected and feared Patton more than any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.280 --> 00:31:01.280
other Allied commander. Thus, Patton was placed&nbsp;
in charge of a fictitious US army group, as part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:08.720
of the Allied plan was to deceive the Germans&nbsp;
as to where and when they would invade France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:16.560
This was the 1st US Army Group or FUSAG, and&nbsp;
the leaked intelligence on the positioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:22.560
and communications of the fake army convinced&nbsp;
Berlin that the Allied landing would occur at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:29.360
Pas-de-Calais, when in reality, the chosen&nbsp;
Allied landing spot was further west on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.360 --> 00:31:36.560
beaches of Normandy. Once the decoy attempt had&nbsp;
been undertaken, Patton was redeployed and given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:45.840
command of a real force in France, the US 3rd&nbsp;
Army. He arrived to France on the 6th July 1944,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:53.280
a month after the initial D-Day landings. Patton’s&nbsp;
mission was to take his force west to capture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.280 --> 00:32:00.640
the ports of Brittany. On the 1st of August&nbsp;
1944, with his armoured units leading the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.640 --> 00:32:07.600
Patton stormed through the northwestern corner of&nbsp;
France at breakneck speed, quickly overrunning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:14.240
province. The speed with which he carried out this&nbsp;
exercise indicated that Patton knew that Brittany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.240 --> 00:32:21.200
was only a sideshow in the war. He wanted to get&nbsp;
back into the thick of the action in trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:29.680
liberate Paris and then pushing on into Belgium&nbsp;
and eastern France. Thus, with Brittany subdued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:40.160
Patton reoriented his forces eastwards and on&nbsp;
the 3rd of August began his lightning advance.
&nbsp;

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:46.800
When battling through the German defences&nbsp;
at Normandy in June, advancing a mile a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:54.320
was considered good progress for the Allies.&nbsp;
However, Patton demanded that his fast-moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.320 --> 00:33:01.360
armoured divisions advance upwards of 50&nbsp;
miles a day. In this way he was soon able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:08.160
to turn back eastwards from Brittany and he&nbsp;
and his 3rd Army played an important role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:15.360
the trapping of tens of thousands of German&nbsp;
soldiers in the Falaise pocket in mid-August.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:22.720
Patton was greatly assisted by air support during&nbsp;
his French campaign, whose reconnaissance patrols,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:29.680
informed Patton’s forces of enemy positions, and&nbsp;
whose bombers provided protection and support for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:36.960
his armoured columns. Additionally, air support&nbsp;
was incredibly valuable for Patton in securing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:45.200
flanks, as during his lightning advances, Patton’s&nbsp;
lines often became long and thin, making the rear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:52.880
of his forces vulnerable to enemy counterattacks.&nbsp;
With the Battle of Normandy virtually over after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:52.880 --> 00:34:00.160
the capture of the Falaise pocket, Patton wished&nbsp;
to advance on Paris, but he was directed to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.160 --> 00:34:06.800
southeast towards Lorraine in eastern France,&nbsp;
thus allowing the Free French forces to liberate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:15.520
their own capital on the 25th of August 1944.&nbsp;
Patton’s eastwards offensive came to a halt at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:23.360
the end of August as supplies, in particular&nbsp;
fuel, began to dwindle. He was extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:29.440
frustrated by this and criticised Montgomery&nbsp;
and other Allied commanders, claiming he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:40.960
being hampered by the slow movement of others.
The autumn of 1944 was frustrating for Patton. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:46.800
forces were battered by heavy rain and met&nbsp;
stern resistance from the Germans, who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:53.840
been preparing the border defences between France&nbsp;
and Germany since D-Day. There was, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.840 --> 00:35:00.880
staunch resistance met in the city of Metz,&nbsp;
which didn’t fall until the 22nd of November.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:07.920
Ever the military history buff, Patton commented&nbsp;
to his staff upon the fall of Metz that it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:15.440
first time in over 400 years, that the city, so&nbsp;
often a battleground in Europe’s violent history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:22.800
had been taken by direct assault. There was&nbsp;
a touch of hyperbole here. The city had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:28.320
captured by the Germans in 1870 during the&nbsp;
early stages of the Franco-Prussian War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:37.040
though that was after a two-month long siege. By&nbsp;
early December 1944, Patton’s supply situation was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:44.720
improving, as the capture of the port of Antwerp&nbsp;
by the Allies in September 1944 had allowed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:51.840
increased supply from Britain to the continent.&nbsp;
He consequently became central to the planning for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:59.760
launching a winter offensive aimed at Frankfurt&nbsp;
and the River Rhine. However, in mid-December the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:06.560
Nazis launched a desperate counterattack through&nbsp;
the Ardennes forest with the goal of retaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:14.000
Antwerp and slowing the Allies advance into&nbsp;
Germany. Remarkably, Patton and his staff officers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:20.320
were the only major Allied command unit to see&nbsp;
that the Germans were preparing to counterattack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:28.400
in advance of the assault with quarter of a&nbsp;
million men on the 16th of December 1944. Due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:34.640
his prior intelligence of the assault, Patton was&nbsp;
already planning an attack on the southern flank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:40.720
of the bulge that the German advance created&nbsp;
in the Allied lines through their offensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:40.720 --> 00:36:47.760
He thus informed Eisenhower, that he could engage&nbsp;
the German flank and Patton’s troops duly turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:55.440
north to smash into the flanks of the advancing&nbsp;
Axis army. Patton’s 3rd Army pushed on in the days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.440 --> 00:37:02.640
that followed to the Belgian town of Bastogne,&nbsp;
which they reached on the 26th of December. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:10.560
the US 101st Airborne Division had been surrounded&nbsp;
by the Germans. Patton’s move opened up a supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:16.640
corridor for the besieged Allied forces at&nbsp;
Bastogne. Patton’s incredible manoeuvre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:21.360
diverting two-thirds of his army from the&nbsp;
front line and racing to the German flank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:28.000
within two days, helped the Allies quickly turn&nbsp;
the tide of the Battle of the Bulge, preventing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:35.040
the Germans from reaching the River Meuse. His&nbsp;
rapid and decisive manoeuvres in response to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:45.360
assault contrasted with the slow response and&nbsp;
lack of foresight of the other US generals.
&nbsp;

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:52.160
Patton was the only Allied commander, who came&nbsp;
out of the Ardennes Offensive, with his reputation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:59.520
enhanced. He was consequently given greater&nbsp;
resources in the spring offensive into Germany.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:07.520
Patton’s Palatinate offensive began on the 12th&nbsp;
of March 1945. During it, his aggressiveness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.520 --> 00:38:14.000
allowed him to exploit the weakened Wehrmacht,&nbsp;
pushing his armoured divisions in between gaps in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:21.440
the German lines. The 3rd Army made spectacular&nbsp;
gains along the Rhine throughout March, taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:28.720
some 100,000 prisoners. Though Field Marshal&nbsp;
Montgomery was organising an immense operation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:35.440
to cross the Rhine River with considerable&nbsp;
artillery and air support, on the 23rd of March,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.440 --> 00:38:40.960
with Patton’s forces already attempting a&nbsp;
crossing after building a pontoon bridge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:47.920
‘Old Blood and Guts’ phoned General Omar Bradley,&nbsp;
commander of the 12th army group and announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:55.200
that, quote, “You can tell the world, Third&nbsp;
Army made it before Monty.” Patton clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.200 --> 00:39:01.600
looked down on the cautious, methodical British&nbsp;
commander, whose operations he perceived as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:09.280
allocated too many Allied resources. Despite his&nbsp;
successes in Germany, Patton could be prone to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:16.160
errors of judgement. For example, he sent a force&nbsp;
to liberate a prisoner camp near Hammelburg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.160 --> 00:39:22.480
40 miles behind enemy lines, on the 26th of&nbsp;
March, having learned that his son-in-law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:30.640
was held captive there. This force was captured by&nbsp;
the Germans, while the camp was subsequently freed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:37.360
a week later by other US forces, making&nbsp;
the operation a complete waste of men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:37.360 --> 00:39:44.880
Annoyance at this setback turned to horror as&nbsp;
Patton and his men advanced further into Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:54.720
and liberated the concentration camp at Ohrdurf in&nbsp;
April 1945. Even ‘Old Blood and Guts’, as tough as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.720 --> 00:40:01.680
he was, was shaken by what he witnessed there.&nbsp;
After a rapid advance through southern Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:09.680
against an exhausted Wehrmacht, Patton reached&nbsp;
the Czechoslovak border on the 4th of May. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:15.360
wanted to immediately push on to Prague before&nbsp;
the war ended and for the Allies to take part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:21.440
in the capture of Berlin in order to limit the&nbsp;
Soviet sphere of influence in Central Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.440 --> 00:40:28.560
however Eisenhower rejected this plan as it ran&nbsp;
counter to the terms of the Yalta conference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:35.040
where the Allied leaders had agreed that most of&nbsp;
Eastern Germany and Eastern Europe would become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.040 --> 00:40:44.560
Soviet spheres of influence after the war.
Nazi Germany surrendered to the Allies on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:53.840
7th of May 1945, bringing the Second World War to&nbsp;
an end in Europe, although VE day is celebrated on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.840 --> 00:41:01.120
the 8th of May. At that stage, Patton and the 3rd&nbsp;
Army had been in active combat since the 1st of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:11.440
August 1944, a span of 281 days. Indeed, Patton’s&nbsp;
forces captured more land and prisoners than any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.440 --> 00:41:18.800
other Allied army in the European theatre. The&nbsp;
Allied invasion of Europe was thus the peak of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.800 --> 00:41:27.440
Patton’s military career, his finest campaign.&nbsp;
However, it would also be his last. Following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:27.440 --> 00:41:34.560
the German surrender, Patton returned home on the&nbsp;
7th of June 1945, for a brief period of leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:41.600
during which time he spoke at huge gatherings&nbsp;
of admirers and took part in military parades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:47.760
He also lobbied for a command in the Pacific&nbsp;
theatre of war, where the US was still fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:54.160
Japan. Instead, after returning to Europe,&nbsp;
he was appointed as military governor of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.160 --> 00:42:02.880
German state of Bavaria, a political role that the&nbsp;
maverick Patton was not at all suited for. Once in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.880 --> 00:42:10.320
that position he attracted controversy with his&nbsp;
outspoken anti-Soviet and anti-Semitic remarks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:16.640
What most angered the US high command was his&nbsp;
tolerance of former Nazis in political posts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:24.480
across local government in Bavaria. Furthermore,&nbsp;
Patton openly stated what most Allied commanders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:31.600
already knew, but did not wish to say aloud,&nbsp;
that post-war Germany could not be properly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:37.600
administered without the support of some&nbsp;
former Nazis, as there had been millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:43.840
of active members of the party. Many Allied&nbsp;
commanders probably partially empathised with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:51.280
inflammatory statements about continuing the war&nbsp;
and invading the Soviet Union as well, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:51.280 --> 00:43:01.120
were anathema to the Allied high command.  
As the crisis over Patton’s statements continued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:01.120 --> 00:43:07.360
he was relieved of his military governorship&nbsp;
by Eisenhower in late September. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.360 --> 00:43:15.120
on the 7th of October 1945, he was relieved of the&nbsp;
command of the 3rd Army as well, causing Patton to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:22.480
remark at the change of command that “All good&nbsp;
things must come to an end. The best thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:29.840
has ever happened to me thus far, is the honour&nbsp;
and privilege, of having commanded the 3rd Army.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:29.840 --> 00:43:37.920
Patton remained in Europe as commander of the 15th&nbsp;
Army. This was, though, largely an office job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.920 --> 00:43:44.640
as the 15th Army was tasked with writing an&nbsp;
official historical account of the war in Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:51.680
In response to his demotion, Patton considered&nbsp;
retiring from the armed forces altogether.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:51.680 --> 00:44:00.480
However, he never returned home, as on the 9th&nbsp;
of December 1945, shortly before noon, Patton’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:09.680
car collided with a US army truck, causing him&nbsp;
severe injuries including a broken neck. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:17.600
‘Old Blood and Guts’ lingered in hospital for 12&nbsp;
days, he eventually died on the 21st of December&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.600 --> 00:44:26.320
1945 at the age of 60. He was buried, as he had&nbsp;
requested, alongside other soldiers of the 3rd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:37.200
Army, near Luxembourg City in the Ardennes, the&nbsp;
site of one of his finest hours as a soldier.
&nbsp;

00:44:37.200 --> 00:44:44.400
George S. Patton was a giant of American military&nbsp;
history and is one of the most famous Allied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:51.600
commanders of the Second World War, though he&nbsp;
was also one of the most controversial. Despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:59.040
his reputation as a tough, brute force soldier,&nbsp;
Patton was actually a military intellectual and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:06.480
was well read on military history from his youth&nbsp;
onwards. For instance, he regularly contributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:15.040
to military journals on a range of topics. He also&nbsp;
saw where warfare was headed from early on in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.040 --> 00:45:22.160
career. After serving in France during the First&nbsp;
World War, he concluded that armoured divisions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:29.120
tanks and other vehicles would be central to the&nbsp;
armies of the twentieth century. His deployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:35.840
of swift moving armoured divisions like this&nbsp;
made him an American version of Heinz Guderian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.840 --> 00:45:41.760
the German commander who was responsible above&nbsp;
all others for the Blitzkrieg warfare deployed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:49.440
such effect in France and the Low Countries in the&nbsp;
summer of 1940 and against the Soviet Union in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:58.400
first months of the war with the Russians between&nbsp;
June and October 1941. Beyond his tactical skill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:05.120
Patton’s heroic style of leadership, placing&nbsp;
great emphasis on personal courage, led to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:12.560
perception of him as a great commander, one whom&nbsp;
the Germans genuinely feared. While he served for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:12.560 --> 00:46:20.080
four decades in the armed forces, his legacy was&nbsp;
ultimately carved out through three campaigns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:29.200
in North Africa between November 1942 and May&nbsp;
1943, in Sicily briefly in the summer of 1943,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.200 --> 00:46:38.240
and then in France, the Low Countries and&nbsp;
Germany between July 1944 and May 1945.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:45.760
In each of these spheres he made his fellow&nbsp;
Allied commanders look like subpar generals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.760 --> 00:46:52.080
winning striking victories and advancing at great&nbsp;
speed against the Germans and Italians where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.080 --> 00:47:00.480
others crept slowly forward. Patton’s character&nbsp;
was deeply paradoxical and controversial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:07.760
He was a highly religious man, but one who had a&nbsp;
dubious personal life and who often behaved in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:15.440
vulgar manner. His post-war correspondence while&nbsp;
governing Bavaria also leaves little doubt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:22.960
he was quite anti-Semitic. Nevertheless, his&nbsp;
unique character and command style has ensured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:30.960
him an enduring place in American popular&nbsp;
culture. The 1970 film Patton about his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:39.280
starring George C. Scott as ‘Old Blood and Guts’,&nbsp;
won seven academy awards and helped cement his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:39.280 --> 00:47:48.960
reputation as one of the most influential and&nbsp;
fascinating figures of the Second World War.
&nbsp;

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:55.760
What do you think of George S Patton? Was he an&nbsp;
unstable warmonger who would have continued the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.760 --> 00:48:02.720
war by attacking the Soviet Union if he had&nbsp;
his own way, or was he a military genius who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.720 --> 00:48:10.000
surpassed all of America’s other generals,&nbsp;
including MacArthur and Eisenhower? Please&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:19.280
let us know in the comment section and in the&nbsp;
meantime, thank you very much for watching.

